{
  "dom_id": "step-q332726",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}-4y=9{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{\u2032} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{4x})^{\u2032} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{4x})^{\u2032} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\u2032}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x})-4\\alpha x{e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ (1+4x-4x)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=9x{e}^{4x}.\n$$",
